+# BEAM v0.23 Ascend adaptation
+
+## Approved scope and development boundary
+
+Add Ascend support alongside the existing NVIDIA path, using the DSMI clock/power primitives in `~/Projects/parsa/code/src/freq.py` and the later Ascend changes in `setup/beam-vllm.patch`.
+Do not reapply the 19-commit patch series, replace the scheduler, or add its unrelated PARSA KV projection.
+The user approved the proposed adaptation and requested code-only work: no commands, imports, tests, test collection, builds, inference, hardware calls, formatting, linting or automated verification are executed during this change.
+No new test suite or commits are requested for this extension.
+
+## Source references
+
+- Existing core base: official vLLM `v0.23.0`, `0fc695fc6d1d82e9a5ac6835ac8e4e1c83703665`, plus the BEAM port on `beam-port-v0.23`.
+- Ascend backend: `vllm-project/vllm-ascend` release `v0.23.0`, source SHA `5cb98caaadeff42b5b62b996e34bb2aaa29d20fd`.
+- Inspected backend files: `vllm_ascend/platform.py`, `worker/worker.py`, `worker/model_runner_v1.py`, platform patch registration and dependency metadata.
+- Inspected `Ascend/pytorch` `v2.10.0` source `torch_npu/npu/streams.py`: `Event(enable_timing=True)`, `record(stream)`, `synchronize()` and `elapsed_time(end)` in milliseconds.
+- Prior patch: Ascend frequency/control changes around lines 88531–89047; power integration around 89502–89542 and 89819–89830; Ascend profile defaults/lookup around 89882–89967.
+
+Standard isolated core builds request Torch 2.11.0 in `pyproject.toml:[build-system]`.
+This is not an unconditional runtime requirement: `setup.py:get_requirements` selects `requirements/common.txt`, without a Torch pin, for `VLLM_TARGET_DEVICE=empty`.
+The supplied `setup/Dockerfile.0.23` uses that target with `--no-build-isolation --no-deps` against Torch 2.10.0 / torch-npu 2.10.0.post4, then installs the Ascend backend separately.
+That distinction avoids the identified build-requirement conflict; it does not establish installation or runtime compatibility.
+No dependency declarations were changed and no installation was performed.
+
+## Bounded implementation contract
+
+- A small `BeamNPUWorker` subclass delegates initialization, model loading, sampling, PP communication and teardown to the released regular Ascend worker.
+- Configuration admits the inspected native scheduler, V1 synchronous eager execution, native single-host uni/mp placement, DP1 and no context parallelism; uni requires TP×PP=1. Alternate Ascend workers/scheduler replacements fail explicitly. The optional, known `benchmarks.energy.worker_tools.BeamToolWorker` RPC extension is admitted; arbitrary worker mixins are not.
+- Synchronous PP retains the existing BEAM ownership, FIFO and real-empty-invocation protocol. Async NPU PP/V2 are not inferred from the CUDA sample-carry proof.
+- Each lazy DSMI controller owns one physical device. A rank-to-DSMI mapping or explicit `ASCEND_RT_VISIBLE_DEVICES` mapping is required; ambiguity, mismatch and unavailable devices fail instead of falling back to device zero.
+- Clock changes occur at the worker's real-batch boundary, synchronously, with checked ABI return values. Requested, observed and rated MHz are different values. Cleanup restores the same device's rated frequency, not an unobservable prior lock or automatic-DVFS state.
+- Ascend profile settings preserve the historical 900–1650 MHz catalog, 512-token default, 1650 MHz defaults and full-profile ceiling lookup without changing NVIDIA defaults.
+- NPU timing is labeled as the device-event interval around the native runner model-call region, not guaranteed isolated kernel time or GPU-equivalent forward timing. Warmup/dummy calls outside actual scheduled work are not recorded.
+- Ascend energy is timestamped sampled-power trapezoidal integration, with explicit units and coverage/errors. It is not an NVML cumulative hardware counter. Missing observations never become zero-energy success.
+- Native SLO/manual controls and fidelity propagation remain unchanged. Benchmark identity, logs and analysis must distinguish NPU estimates from GPU counters.
+
+## Implementation sequence
+
+1. Add lazy, checked DSMI controller, explicit device resolution, NPU profiler and narrow worker wrapper.
+2. Add configuration/CLI propagation, device-profile selection and compatible native-owned profiling admission.
+3. Adapt the existing benchmark/profile readers and worker RPC identities for the separate NPU schema.
+4. Manually inspect definitions, callers, failure cleanup, device ownership and all changed branches. Record unsupported combinations and runtime-dependent uncertainty; execute nothing.
+
+## Implemented source
+
+| Area | Files / behavior |
+|---|---|
+| DSMI controller | `vllm/v1/worker/beam_ascend.py`: lazy checked ctypes binding, one physical ID, explicit visibility/map agreement, current/rated MHz, instantaneous Watts, successful-request caching and same-device rated-frequency restoration |
+| Native worker integration | `beam_ascend_worker.py`: `BeamNPUWorker` subclasses the released regular worker, keeps native initialization/PP/sampling, consumes finalized batch clocks and wraps only its runner instance's model-call method during real scheduled profiling |
+| NPU measurement | `beam_ascend_profiler.py`: deferred NPU event timing and separately labeled power samples/window estimates, retained failed data, frozen successful export windows and confirmed sampler quiescence before restoration/teardown |
+| Configuration / CLI | `config/parallel.py`, `config/vllm.py`, `engine/arg_utils.py`: `beam_npu_device_map`, `beam_npu_dsmi_library`, strict validation/propagation and explicit worker/backend capability gates |
+| Policy inputs | `core/sched/energy_model.py`, `dynamo_energy_model.py`, `beam_scheduler.py`: NPU defaults and lookup behavior; invalid historical rows filtered before metric parsing; NPU Dynamo metadata/catalog checked at ingestion |
+| Owned profiling | `v1/engine/beam_profile.py`: admits the validated NPU wrapper using the existing native request/KV lifecycle and reports backend/measurement kind separately |
+| Tooling | Existing `benchmarks/energy` workers, collectors, mixed profiles, monitoring, generation/resume and plotting support disjoint NPU identity/file/domain contracts; GPU default formats remain intact |
+
+Physical map CLI input is a JSON object such as `--beam-npu-device-map '{"0": 2, "1": 3}'`, keyed by global TP/PP rank, not a filename.
+If no map is provided, an explicit numeric `ASCEND_RT_VISIBLE_DEVICES` list is required; both sources must agree when present.
+Use `VLLM_USE_V2_MODEL_RUNNER=0`, explicit synchronous scheduling and eager execution with the pinned backend. These are future deployment settings, not settings or commands executed here.
+The old `beam_gpu_name` argument remains the backward-compatible device-profile identity, including names such as `910B4`.
+
+The NPU clock catalog is `900,1000,1100,1200,1300,1400,1500,1650` MHz; system-profile default coverage is 512 tokens at 1650 MHz.
+Historical NPU `valid=0/0.0/False/false` rows are excluded before numeric parsing, including default coverage; unknown markers fail. A missing validity column retains legacy rows.
+NPU Dynamo profiles must explicitly declare `device_backend=npu`, `energy_domain=sampled_power_trapezoid_device_window` and `estimator=trapezoidal_power_w`; all candidate clocks are checked before serving. Untagged legacy Dynamo rows remain CUDA by default.
+
+NPU log families are `batch_log_NPU_R.csv`, `power_log_NPU_R.csv`, `energy_window_NPU_R.csv`, `dvfs_log_NPU_R.csv` and `connector_log_NPU_R.csv`.
+They do not pretend a power integral is an NVML hardware counter or an NPU integer ID is a GPU UUID.
+Detailed options, file headers and later-only examples are in `benchmarks/energy/REPRODUCE.md`.
+
+## Manual review and remaining limits
+
+Manual review traced definitions and consumers for configuration, device mapping, clock intent, profile lookup, native worker delegation, sampled energy, log readers and cleanup.
+Corrections include upfront NPU Dynamo clock/domain validation, historical invalid-row handling, rejecting unused NPU settings on CUDA/disabled BEAM, uni topology validation, preserving primary profiling errors, and sampler-exit confirmation across interrupted cleanup.
+No test files were added or executed, and no command, import, build, linter, formatter, inference or hardware API was run for this extension. No new commits were created.
+
+- Implemented support is synchronous V1 eager text-decoder generation, native uni/mp, single host, DP1 and no context/expert parallelism. Synchronous TP/PP is source-supported, not hardware-verified.
+- Async/V2, alternate Ascend workers/schedulers, ACL graphs, ubatching/DBO, MoE/hybrid/SSM, speculation/LoRA and NPU disaggregation/KV connectors are explicitly unsupported in this candidate.
+- The wrapper checks pinned distribution versions; the experiment harness additionally requires the exact clean Ascend source checkout. Neither is an execution-based compatibility test.
+- DSMI ABI/header compatibility, permissions, real launcher/container mapping, actual frequencies, PP/HCCL progress, installation and numerical calibration remain unverified.
+- Energy is a whole-device sampled-window estimate; it includes host/PP/sample overhead and other device activity. Missing/invalid/gapped observations reject export, not zero-fill. Raw logs do not include the sampler interval, so readers cannot independently reconstruct its maximum-gap policy.
+- A native DSMI or NPU event call can block. Unconfirmed sampler exit blocks restoration/native teardown rather than claiming cleanup; forced process loss still cannot guarantee restoration.
+- Restoring rated MHz does not restore a prior unobservable lock or automatic-DVFS mode. Exclusive physical-device ownership is required.
+
+This is a source implementation candidate, not a claim of runtime correctness, hardware support, installation compatibility, profile calibration, SLO compliance or measured energy savings.
