+## Ascend NPU compatibility (source adaptation, not an executed reproduction)
+
+This extension changes only `beam_tools.py`, `worker_tools.py`, `analysis.py`,
+`mixed_profile.py`, `benchmark_script.py` and this document. No new tests were
+written, and no commands, Python/imports, algorithms, collection, scripts, builds,
+formatting, lint, installation, GPU/NPU/CANN/DSMI/NVML operations or automated
+checks were executed during this extension. The existing CUDA measurement fields,
+UUID identities, numeric Dynamo schema, defaults and authentication are retained.
+
+### Backend, process boundary and source pins
+
+`offline-profile`, `generation-grid`, `mixed-profile`, `run` and `dynamo-sweep`
+accept `--device-backend {cuda,npu}` (default `cuda`),
+`--beam-npu-device-map '{"0": 0, "1": 1}'` and
+`--beam-npu-dsmi-library /absolute/path/to/libdrvdsmi_host.so`.
+The map and library options require the explicit NPU backend. The map is JSON,
+not a filename: every global TP/PP rank must have a unique nonnegative physical
+DSMI integer ID. EngineArgs receives integer keys/values; server CLI receives
+the equivalent JSON under the existing `--beam-npu-device-map` flag.
+The library path is forwarded unchanged and loaded only by the assigned worker.
+Absent a map, the worker requires an explicit, valid
+`ASCEND_RT_VISIBLE_DEVICES` mapping. When both exist they must agree with the
+assigned logical device. Neither a rank nor a Torch logical index silently
+becomes DSMI device zero.
+
+The NPU branch supplies `worker_cls=vllm.v1.worker.beam_ascend_worker.BeamNPUWorker`,
+`enforce_eager=True`, `async_scheduling=False` and native local `mp` placement.
+The generated serve command therefore includes `--enforce-eager` and
+`--no-async-scheduling`. The bounded native contract is V1 synchronous eager,
+native Scheduler, single-host uni/mp, DP1, no context/expert parallelism,
+ubatching/DBO, MoE/SSM, speculation, LoRA, disaggregation or KV/EC connectors.
+Unsupported settings fail in the existing core/worker validators; the tooling
+does not rewrite global runner flags or enable another platform as a fallback.
+
+For in-process/offline/mixed generation, set `VLLM_USE_V2_MODEL_RUNNER=0`
+**before starting Python**. Missing or different values are rejected with that
+guidance. An owned `run` child (including campaign and Dynamo sweep cases) gets
+the explicit intended value `0` in its child environment, also for its binary
+version probe. `manifest.json` records `command_environment` for that value,
+the required environment and source/package provenance, without recording the
+private authentication token. The parent environment is not silently changed.
+Campaign case and pipeline step argument arrays carry the same backend/map/
+library options; offline pipeline steps still require the outer Python process
+to start with the V1 environment selection.
+
+`check_target(..., device_backend="npu")` retains the original core/artifact
+gates and additionally requires:
+
+- Installed `vllm-ascend==0.23.0`, Torch `2.10.0`, and
+  `torch-npu==2.10.0.post4` (local `+...` build metadata is allowed).
+- The import-resolved `vllm_ascend` package must come from a clean, inspectable
+  Git checkout at exact SHA `5cb98caaadeff42b5b62b996e34bb2aaa29d20fd`.
+  Version-only wheels without this source evidence are rejected by this harness.
+- The actual selected vLLM platform must have `device_type='npu'`; actual RPC
+  identities must also report NPU workers. `BeamNPUWorker` independently checks
+  the pinned distribution versions before importing the native Ascend worker.
+
+**Build/runtime distinction:** isolated core builds request Torch **2.11.0** in
+`pyproject.toml:[build-system]`; the `VLLM_TARGET_DEVICE=empty` branch of
+`setup.py:get_requirements` selects common runtime requirements without a Torch
+pin. The supplied Ascend Docker recipe uses that target and
+`--no-build-isolation --no-deps` with **Torch 2.10.0 / torch-npu 2.10.0.post4**.
+This avoids the identified build-requirement conflict, but is not proof of
+installability or runtime compatibility. No installation was performed and no
+CUDA dependency was changed by this extension.
+
+Both core and `BeamNPUWorker` admit exactly
+`benchmarks.energy.worker_tools.BeamToolWorker` as the optional tooling mixin;
+other worker-extension mixins remain rejected. NPU `run` uses this admitted
+extension for RPC identity, clock control and monitoring. Offline callable
+identity/clock RPCs and owned mixed profiling do not inject the mixin.
+
+### Physical identities and worker-only controls
+
+- CUDA `beam_tools_identity()` remains exactly `{rank, uuid}`.
+- NPU identity is `{rank, device_identity: "NPU-N", device_id: N, backend: "npu"}`.
+  `worker_identity(worker)` uses the same contract for offline callable RPCs.
+- `rank_device_map(mapping, count)` preserves GPU UUID normalization and accepts
+  physical NPU integers (or explicit canonical `NPU-N` identities, normalized to
+  integers). Maps must cover all ranks without duplicate devices or mixed backends.
+  Every former identity consumer now validates the appropriate identity envelope.
+- NPU `beam_tools_clock(request)` validates the intended physical integer or
+  canonical `NPU-N` identity before actuation; it never parses an NPU as a UUID.
+  Fixed-clock B uses `--device-identities NPU-0 NPU-1 ...` in rank order and
+  `--clock MHZ`; CUDA B retains `--device-uuids`. Null clock resets the same
+  assigned device. NPU reset restores **rated MHz**, not a previous lock or an
+  inferred automatic-DVFS state. Requested and observed MHz may differ.
+- NPU samples include identity/backend, UTC `timestamp`, midpoint `monotonic_s`,
+  instantaneous `power_w`, observed `clock_mhz`, and
+  `measurement_domain='sampled_power_trapezoid_device_window'`.
+  They contain **no** `uuid` or `total_energy_mj`. DSMI is never loaded on the
+  driver. CUDA sample fields remain unchanged.
+
+Examples below are **later-only invocation descriptions**, not installation or
+hardware validation instructions that were run. Model/token/device choices need
+to match the eventual reviewed deployment:
+
+```bash
+VLLM_USE_V2_MODEL_RUNNER=0 .venv/bin/python -m benchmarks.energy.beam_tools offline-profile \
+  --device-backend npu --beam-npu-device-map '{"0": 0}' \
+  --artifact-root ../BEAM-artifact --model Qwen/Qwen2.5-14B \
+  --tp 1 --pp 1 --clocks 900 1650 --token-id 100 \
+  --input-len 512 --output-len 64 --batch-size 8 --budget 2048 \
+  --output /absolute/new-npu-generation
+
+.venv/bin/python -m benchmarks.energy.beam_tools run \
+  --device-backend npu --beam-npu-device-map '{"0": 0}' \
+  --beam-npu-dsmi-library /usr/local/Ascend/driver/lib64/driver/libdrvdsmi_host.so \
+  --artifact-root ../BEAM-artifact --vllm-bin "$PWD/.venv/bin/vllm" \
+  --model Qwen/Qwen2.5-14B --tp 1 --pp 1 --mode B \
+  --clock 1650 --device-identities NPU-0 --rates 1 --num-prompts 30 \
+  --monitor-seconds 30 --output /absolute/new-npu-fixed-clock
+```
+
+The second example uses the exact admitted tooling mixin. It retains
+the loopback server, whole-server private auth, isolated token, clock cleanup and
+owned-child lifecycle. No NVML support for NPU is implied. The CUDA/NCCL P2P
+connector proof is not an HCCL proof; NPU connector/disaggregation execution stays
+unsupported even though the neutral connector log format can be read.
+
+### Separate NPU profiling and output domains
+
+The worker writes these disjoint rank families (R is a global rank, not a device ID):
+
+| File | Fields |
+|---|---|
+| `batch_log_NPU_R.csv` | `step,phase,elapsed_time,batch_size,clock_mhz,num_reqs,start_time,current_time,num_scheduled_tokens,requested_clock_mhz,timing_domain,energy_domain,device_id` |
+| `power_log_NPU_R.csv` | `current_time,monotonic_s,power_w,clock_mhz,device_id` |
+| `energy_window_NPU_R.csv` | `start_monotonic_s,end_monotonic_s,energy_mj,sample_count,estimator,energy_domain,device_id` (exactly one row) |
+| `dvfs_log_NPU_R.csv` | `current_time,clock_mhz,num_scheduled_tokens,expected_tbt,requested_clock_mhz,step,phase,device_id` |
+| `connector_log_NPU_R.csv` | `operation,layer,start_time,elapsed_time_s,device_id` |
+
+`timing_domain='npu_event_runner_model_call_ms'` means NPU event milliseconds
+around the native runner's model-call region, including parameter updates and
+possible hidden-state all-gather. It is neither isolated kernel time nor
+`cuda_event_model_forward_ms`. Host wall timestamps are enqueue observations,
+not NPU completion timestamps. Events remain deferred until profiler stop;
+dummy/warmup calls outside actual scheduled profiling work are not recorded.
+
+`energy_domain='sampled_power_trapezoid_device_window'` and
+`estimator='trapezoidal_power_w'` describe the **entire first-to-last valid
+sampled device window**, in mJ. It includes native host/PP/sample overhead and
+other device activity; it is not per-forward attribution or a hardware counter.
+The existing worker synchronizes recorded end events at stop, stops/joins its
+sampler, takes a final observation and freezes a valid window before writing.
+Sampling/event errors and gaps beyond three configured sampling intervals reject export;
+I/O retries retain the same frozen window. Reader validation does not invent a
+sampling cadence: the schema does not carry `interval_s` for independently
+rechecking that worker-side maximum-gap limit.
+
+`summarize_profile` dispatches by file family, rejects mixed CUDA/NPU directories
+and incomplete rank sets, and for NPU checks exact headers, one window row,
+finite ordered sample coverage, sample count, consistent/unique physical IDs,
+domains, clocks and recomputed trapezoidal mJ against declared mJ with floating
+tolerance. Batch/power/window families are mandatory; optional DVFS/connector
+families, when present, must cover every rank. Common `ranks` values
+`energy_j`, `window_seconds`, `window_power_w` and `forwards` are device-neutral
+window/count quantities. NPU timing uses `mean_runner_model_call_ms` and
+`sum_runner_model_call_ms`, with backend/domain/estimator metadata; no NPU
+`mean_forward_ms`, `sum_forward_ms` or CUDA timing alias is manufactured.
+
+NPU real-generation tables use `beam_npu_generation_window_v1`, with the common
+workload/window columns plus `mean_runner_model_call_ms`, `forward_count`,
+`device_id`, `device_identity`, `device_backend`, `energy_domain`, `timing_domain`
+and `estimator`, instead of GPU mean-time/UUID fields. Generation-grid propagates
+backend/map/library options to every point; resume validates the NPU header,
+domains, complete clock×rank coverage, hashes and backend configuration using
+the same atomic publication and post-cleanup completion-marker protocol.
+NPU resume identity also binds the explicit visible-device and DSMI-library
+environment selections; these nonsecret values are recorded in provenance.
+Real-generation collection sets each clock before warmup, then uses one profile
+window around all measured repeats. Each clock point reapplies its requested
+frequency; NPU profile stop restores rated MHz, so a fixed frequency is never
+assumed to survive stop. A failed start, measured generation or stop prevents
+the normal completion-marker publication path.
+
+Owned mixed profiling retains the neutral native `beam_owned_mixed_window_v1`
+contract and exact-coordinate checks, additionally requiring NPU
+`device_backend` and `energy_measurement_kind` from the core. It reads
+`batch_log_NPU_R.csv` and `clock_mhz`, accepts exactly one scheduled model call
+per rank, and emits `runner_model_call_ms` and physical identity fields rather
+than `cuda_forward_ms`/`uuid`. Manifest, dispositions, accepted measurements and
+aggregates retain NPU domain metadata. Each NPU repetition reapplies the clock
+because the preceding profile stop restores rated MHz. No legacy lookup row is
+fabricated; `legacy_lookup_compatible=false` and `calibration_claim=false` remain.
+
+### NPU monitoring, Dynamo and analysis consumers
+
+`run` exports `npu_power_monitor.csv`, a long-form Watts series, and a unit/domain
+sidecar. Standalone `monitor` selects this representation from actual identities.
+The legacy `gpu_energy_monitor.csv` cumulative mJ export rejects NPU samples.
+NPU `power_window(..., mode="linear")` requires complete per-rank monotonic
+coverage and integrates trapezoids, linearly interpolating **power** at the exact
+cutoff. It rejects missing/mixed identities, samples or domains rather than
+substituting zero. NPU `run` uses this mode for `first_30s`; CUDA retains its
+historical/linear cumulative-counter semantics.
+
+NPU `dynamo-collect` and `dynamo-sweep` require `--device-backend npu` and
+`--power-mode linear` for a sampled cutoff window. `dynamo-collect` also permits
+explicit `--power-mode whole-window` using the validated estimated profiler
+window. `dynamo-sweep --devices` accepts rank-to-NPU-integer maps (or per-layout
+maps) and forwards them to worker placement when no separate NPU map is given.
+`dynamo-merge --device-backend npu` preserves NPU domain tags and rejects mixed
+CUDA/NPU inputs. The native selector still requires the legacy numeric field
+name `gpu_power`: on these tagged NPU rows it means **summed estimated device
+window Watts**, not a GPU measurement or cumulative energy. NPU rows append
+`device_backend,energy_domain,estimator`; the native reader consumes only its
+required numeric metrics and separately validates measurement metadata. The
+native `ClockSelector(..., device_backend="cuda")` boundary is authoritative:
+it accepts untagged legacy CUDA profiles, requires all three exact NPU tags for
+`device_backend="npu"`, validates every NPU row's catalog clock at load time,
+and rejects cross-backend/tagged-domain mismatches. B2 harness preflight calls
+this same constructor with the explicit selected backend for both CUDA and NPU,
+before creating its run output directory or child server. NPU B2 and direct NPU
+loader calls require tagged profiles; untagged historical CUDA or NPU Dynamo
+rows are never automatically converted or relabeled. Legacy NPU Dynamo data
+needs separately reviewed explicit metadata and calibration before NPU use.
+
+Comparisons validate backend, energy domain, timing domain and estimator in
+addition to existing workload/cohort controls. CUDA counters and NPU estimates
+cannot form one energy cohort; intentional baseline and TP/PP differences
+within a matching NPU cohort remain allowed. NPU timelines read native batch,
+power, DVFS and connector families, label runner model-call milliseconds,
+integrated **estimated** Joules, instantaneous Watts and NPU MHz, and verify the
+monitor/profile device maps. No NPU event interval is called CUDA-forward time
+or plotted as a reconstructed device completion timeline.
+
+Historical Ascend `freq`-pipeline lookup CSVs with
+`clock,batch_size,total_ctx_len,time_taken,energy_consumption,valid` remain a
+separate domain: `time_taken` is seconds and `energy_consumption` is **mJ**.
+`heatmap --device-backend npu` explicitly supports this legacy schema and its
+valid-row markers (1/0 or true/false), scaling to ms/J, or tagged NPU Dynamo
+profiles with selected TP/PP. It rejects owned/generation window tables and
+unknown column/unit schemas instead of guessing a lookup conversion. NPU
+`overhead` requires an explicit historical profile and NPU identity via the
+legacy `--gpu-name` option; it selects the NPU policy model, not the synthetic
+A100 grid, and passes `device_backend="npu"` to the shared `EnergySimulator`.
+The shared NPU profile reader discards false `valid` rows before parsing their
+numeric metrics, using the same markers as the heatmap reader. An entirely
+invalid table raises `ValueError("Profile contains no data rows")`, before any
+default-coverage check; surviving valid rows still need the native clock/default
+coverage. S1/S2 `run`
+likewise needs a matching legacy profile and explicit
+device-profile identity such as `--gpu-name 910B4`; the core's historical NPU
+catalog/defaults (900–1650 MHz, 512-token policy default, 1650-MHz defaults)
+do not establish hardware capability or recalibration. Harness A/B/B2/B3 keep
+their explicit 256-token experiment ceiling, including B3's fixed-256 policy.
+
+Runtime correctness, installability, DSMI permissions/ABI on the eventual host,
+clock capability, profile calibration and transport behavior remain unverified.
+The existing CUDA test-source descriptions below do not constitute NPU coverage.
